CREATE TABLE CINEMA (
    SEAT_ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    FREE BOOL
);

counterexample:
-- Under the given schema, every test database that respects the constraints produces equivalent results for Q1 and Q2. For example, consider the data below:

INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (2, 0);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (3, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (4, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (5, 0);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (6, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (7, 1);

In this test data:
 • For seat 1: The neighbor seat 0 is absent and seat 2 is occupied, so Q1 does not return 1. (Q2 also does not include seat 1 since it is an isolated free seat in the free-seat ordering.)
 • For seats 3 and 4: Q1 returns both because 3’s right neighbor is 4 and 4’s left neighbor is 3; in Q2 these two form a contiguous group (their computed FLAG is the same) so they are returned.
 • For seats 6 and 7: Similarly, Q1 returns both (they are adjacent) and Q2 returns them as well.

Thus Q1 returns {3,4,6,7} and Q2 also returns {3,4,6,7}.  

Any attempt to create a counterexample (for instance by having gaps in the underlying seat numbers) fails because Q2’s window function is computed only over seats where FREE = 1 and, by definition, two free seats can only be grouped as contiguous if their numeric values differ by 1.  

Therefore, no counterexample exists (under the given schema and constraints) where Q1 and Q2 produce different results.

