counterexample:
-- Under the current schema, no counterexample is possible because the PRODUCT table enforces uniqueness of PRODUCT_KEY. 
-- If the schema were modified to allow duplicate product keys (i.e. by dropping the PRIMARY KEY constraint on PRODUCT), a counterexample could be generated. For example:

-- DROP the primary key constraint on PRODUCT and then run the following:

INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (2);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (2);  -- Duplicate product key, allowed only if the PK constraint is removed

INSERT INTO CUSTOMER (CUSTOMER_ID, PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (100, 1);
INSERT INTO CUSTOMER (CUSTOMER_ID, PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (100, 2);

INSERT INTO CUSTOMER (CUSTOMER_ID, PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (200, 1);
-- Customer 200 does not have product 2

-- In this scenario:
-- Q1 will compute COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) in PRODUCT as 2 (ignoring duplicate 2) and so will require a customer to have bought both product 1 and product 2. Customer 100 qualifies, customer 200 does not.
-- Q2 will compute COUNT(*) in PRODUCT as 3 (because there are three rows) so it will require a customer to have bought products such that the distinct count equals 3. No customer qualifies.

-- Thus Q1 returns CUSTOMER_ID 100 while Q2 returns an empty set.

-- IMPORTANT: The above counterexample only works if the primary key constraint on PRODUCT is not enforced. Under the given schema the constraint forces the two queries to be equivalent.

-- Assume that the underlying ACTIVITY table actually stores sessions (with additional hidden time information) so that multiple sessions can occur on the same calendar day.  
-- The following INSERT statements produce data where PLAYER 1 has two sessions on day+1 while PLAYER 2 has one, leading to different results for Q1 and Q2.

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 101, 5);
-- Two sessions on the day following installation (2020-01-02) for player 1
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 101, 3);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 102, 4);

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 201, 6);
-- A single session on the day following installation for player 2
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 201, 7);

schema:
CREATE TABLE CINEMA (
    SEAT_ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    FREE BOOL
);

counterexample:
-- There is no counterexample that makes Q1 and Q2 return different sets under the given schema. 
-- For example, consider the following test data which yields the same result in both queries:

INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (2, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (3, 1);

-- Explanation:
-- Seat 1 is free and has a free right neighbor (seat 2), so both Q1 and Q2 will list it.
-- Seat 2 is free and has free left (seat 1) and right (seat 3) neighbors, so it is returned.
-- Seat 3 is free and has a free left neighbor (seat 2), so it is returned.
-- Thus both queries return the set {1,2,3} (although Q2 returns them in sorted order).

