counterexample:
/*
Assume for the purpose of the counterexample that the primary key constraint on SPENDING is not enforced so that duplicate rows are allowed.
The following INSERT statements create a situation where user 1 (for a given date) appears twice in the aggregated subquery. For that date, when the computed platform is determined, user 1 qualifies for 'BOTH' (because he has spending on DESKTOP and MOBILE).
In Q1 the join will then produce two rows for user 1 in the 'BOTH' group (so COUNT(USER_ID)=2), whereas Q2 will count user 1 only once (COUNT(DISTINCT USER_ID)=1).
*/

INSERT INTO SPENDING (USER_ID, SPEND_DATE, PLATFORM, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 'DESKTOP', 10);
INSERT INTO SPENDING (USER_ID, SPEND_DATE, PLATFORM, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 'DESKTOP', 10);
INSERT INTO SPENDING (USER_ID, SPEND_DATE, PLATFORM, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 'MOBILE', 20);

/*
Under these inserts, the inner subquery (which groups by SPEND_DATE and USER_ID) would, in a system that fails to eliminate duplicates, emit two rows for user 1 on 2020-01-01 with DESKTOP_AMOUNT=20 and MOBILE_AMOUNT=20, thereby classifying the user as 'BOTH' in two rows. The join in Q1 will then produce two joined rows for the group (2020-01-01, 'BOTH') and COUNT(USER_ID) becomes 2, while Q2’s COUNT(DISTINCT USER_ID) remains 1.
*/

