counterexample:
-- Under the given schema constraints, no valid counterexample exists.
-- Any test data inserted must obey the PRIMARY KEY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE), so it is impossible to have a player with two activities on the same day (including on MIN_DATE + 1) which is the only situation where Q1 and Q2 would differ.

-- For instance, the following INSERTs create data for two players, and both queries return the same fraction (0.50):

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 101, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 101, 3);

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-05', 102, 4);
-- Player 2 does not have an event on '2021-01-06', so is not counted as "active".

-- Both Q1 and Q2 will compute the fraction as 1 active out of 2 players, i.e. 0.50.

-- In summary, due to the PRIMARY KEY constraint the discrepancy that might arise if a player had multiple activities on the day after their first activity is impossible; hence, no counterexample exists under the schema.

