INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('B', 'C');

Both Q1 and Q2 will return:
-- FOLLOWER | NUM
--    A     | 1
--    B     | 2

Any test data that satisfies the primary key constraint (and in which no column is null) makes Q1 and Q2 equivalent.

-- The following INSERT statements assume a relaxed version of the ACTIVITY table where duplicates on the same day are allowed
-- (for example, if the table’s primary key were (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATETIME) rather than (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE)).

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 100, 5);
-- Player 1 logs in on the install day
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 100, 3);
-- Player 1 returns on day+1 (one record)

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-01', 101, 2);
-- Player 2 logs in on the install day
-- Player 2 has two separate retention events on day+1 (allowed if time were part of the key)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-02', 101, 4);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-02', 102, 6);

/*
Under these data, Q1 will process player 1 with a single matching day+1 row so his retention contributes 1, while player 2 will contribute 2 (because his install row from 2021-01-01 joins with two separate 2021-01-02 rows). Therefore, in the group for INSTALL_DT = '2021-01-01':
  - The denominator (COUNT of distinct PLAYER_ID) = 2
  - The numerator (SUM of CASE results) = 1 + 2 = 3
  - Resulting DAY1_RETENTION = ROUND(3/2,2) = 1.50
If the intended retention is binary—i.e. a player can only be counted once for returning—then Q1 (which is summing all matching retention rows) yields a different result from a version of Q2 that ensures a player’s retention is at most 1.
*/

-- Under the given schema it is impossible to construct valid test data that distinguishes the two queries because the PRIMARY KEY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE) prevents the tie scenario (multiple rows with the same player and the same event_date) that would be needed for the queries to produce different results.
-- If the schema were modified to allow duplicate EVENT_DATE values per PLAYER_ID (for example, by removing or changing the primary key constraint), a potential counterexample could be constructed as follows:

