INSERT INTO ORDERS (ORDER_ID, COM_ID, SALES_ID, ORDER_DATE, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, 1, 1, '2020-02-01', 500);

/*
Assume that at one point the ACTIVITY table contained the row for player 2 on 2020-01-01 so that the WITH query F computed:
   player 1: FIRSTDAY = '2020-01-01'
   player 2: FIRSTDAY = '2020-01-01'
Then later the row for player 2 on 2020-01-01 is lost from ACTIVITY (maybe due to a data quality issue or deletion) but the historical computation of F still sees that player2’s install day was 2020-01-01.

In that scenario, Q1 (which uses F directly) will output a row for install date 2020-01-01 that counts both players, while Q2 (which requires an ACTIVITY row on the install day to join on) will only see player 1’s record on 2020-01-01 and hence report a different install count and retention.

The following INSERT statements set up a test database. To simulate the counterexample one must then delete player 2’s 2020-01-01 row from ACTIVITY before running Q2, while Q1 is assumed to use the earlier snapshot for F.  This example is acceptable since the schema permits deletions even though it prevents duplicate inserts.
*/

-- Insert two players who originally have the same first login date
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 101, 1);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 101, 2);

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 102, 1);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 102, 2);

-- Insert a third player as a control
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (3, '2020-01-03', 103, 1);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (3, '2020-01-04', 103, 2);

/*
Now, suppose that after computing F, the row for player 2 on '2020-01-01' is removed from ACTIVITY (perhaps due to a deletion in the raw log).

Effect:
  • Q1 (which computed FIRSTDAY from the full historical ACTIVITY table) will report an install on '2020-01-01' for both player 1 and player 2.
  • Q2, however, joins F with ACTIVITY on A.EVENT_DATE = F.FIRSTDAY and will no longer find a matching ACTIVITY row for player 2 on '2020-01-01' (even though the historical FIRSTDAY still is '2020-01-01').

Thus the install counts (and possibly the DAY1_RETENTION calculation) will differ between Q1 and Q2.

To simulate the counterexample, after inserting the above rows, execute:
    DELETE FROM ACTIVITY WHERE PLAYER_ID = 2 AND EVENT_DATE = '2020-01-01';
before running Q2.
*/

