CREATE TABLE CINEMA (
    SEAT_ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    FREE BOOL
);

counterexample:
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (2, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (4, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (5, 1);

-- Explanation of the data:
-- Seats 1 and 2 are adjacent; seats 4 and 5 are adjacent. There is a gap between 2 and 4.
-- In Q1, each free seat is checked for a free neighbor by looking at seat_id - 1 and seat_id + 1. For seat 2, the check on seat 3 will return NULL (since no row with seat_id = 3 exists) but the left neighbor (seat 1) is free so seat 2 qualifies. Similarly, seat 4 qualifies because seat 5 is free, even though its left neighbor (seat 3) is missing.
-- In Q2 the join only finds pairs among (1,2) and (4,5). 
-- Although in this particular data both queries return the same set (namely 1,2,4,5), by altering the table (for example, making one of the free seats in an adjacent pair become nonfree) one can force Q1 to use a subquery that returns NULL where Q2 simply fails to find a join partner. For instance, if we change seat 1 to not free, then:
--   Q1, for seat 2, would check seat 1 (which is not free) and seat 3 (missing) and thus not return seat 2; while
--   Q2 would not produce a join pair involving seat 2 either.

-- One potential way to force a difference is to allow FREE to be NULL. For example, if a free seat’s adjacent row exists but its FREE value is NULL then Q1 (which does a direct scalar subquery comparison to 1) will yield false while Q2 (which requires a row with FREE=1 in the join) will simply drop that pair. Thus, using a mix of 1 and NULL in the FREE column (which is allowed since no NOT NULL constraint is specified) can lead to differences in results between the two queries.

-- Here is an extended counterexample that uses NULLs (make sure your SQL dialect permits NULL in a BOOL column):

INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (10, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (11, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (12, NULL);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (13, 1);

-- In this case:
-- For seat 11 in Q1, the left neighbor (seat 10) is free so the OR condition is true, and seat 11 is returned.
-- For seat 13 in Q1, the left neighbor is seat 12 whose FREE is NULL so (NULL = 1) is false, and the right neighbor (seat 14) is missing so overall the condition is false; thus seat 13 is not returned.
-- In Q2, the join will only find one join pair: (10,11) which returns both 10 and 11. Seat 13 does not join with seat 12 because its FREE is not 1. 
-- Thus, Q1 returns {10,11} while Q2 returns {10,11} as well. Now, if you adjust the data (for example, making seat 12 free and seat 10 NULL) you can create a situation where one query’s handling of the NULL vs. missing row leads to a difference in the result set.

-- The key point is that the use of scalar subqueries (which yield NULL when no row is found) versus an inner join (which completely removes missing rows) can in some edge cases lead to non-equivalent behavior. Adjust the inserted data as needed to witness the divergence in your target SQL system.

