counterexample:
-- Under the given schema with PRIMARY KEY (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER), no counterexample exists. If the constraint were removed, one potential counterexample could be:
-- INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('X', 'Y');
-- INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('X', 'Y');
-- In that case, Q1 would count 2 rows for followee 'X' while Q2 would count 1 distinct follower, but these INSERTs violate the primary key constraint.

-- Under the given schema and constraints there is no valid counterexample.  
-- Any test data inserted (with non‐NULL SALE_DATE values or even with some NULLs) will cause both queries to return the same set of products.

-- For completeness, here is a sample test data set that shows the intended behavior (both queries return the same result):

INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (1, 'InRangeProduct', 100);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (2, 'MixedProduct', 150);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (3, 'OutOfRangeProduct', 200);

-- For PRODUCT 1, all sales are between '2019-01-01' and '2019-03-31'.
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (1, 10, 100, '2019-01-15', 5, 100);
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (1, 11, 101, '2019-03-01', 3, 100);

-- For PRODUCT 2, there is one sale in range and one sale out of range. Neither query returns PRODUCT 2.
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (2, 12, 102, '2019-02-10', 2, 150);
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (2, 13, 103, '2019-04-05', 1, 150);

-- For PRODUCT 3, all sales are outside of the spring window.
