counterexample:
-- The following INSERT statements create the counterexample scenario. 
-- Note: In a production system the PRIMARY KEY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE) would prevent duplicate day records for the same player. 
-- For the purpose of this counterexample, assume that the constraint is either relaxed or disabled.

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 100, 5);
-- Two separate day1 records for the same player (simulate a logging error)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 101, 3);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 102, 4);

-- Also, add a second player with a normal single record on day1 to show that normally the calculations agree for other players
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 200, 6);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 201, 2);

/*
Assuming that for testing purposes we can insert duplicate activity records for the same player and date (i.e. if the PK constraint were relaxed), then the following INSERT statements form a counterexample. Under the declared schema these INSERTs would normally be rejected, so this counterexample shows that the queries are not equivalent in a more relaxed scenario.
*/

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 100, 5);
-- Two records on the day after the first event for player 1 (duplicate dates, different device IDs)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 101, 10);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 102, 12);

-- Player 2 with only one activity (thus no qualifying next-day event)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-03', 103, 7);

/*
For these data:
• For player 1, the MIN_DATE is '2020-01-01'. There are two rows with EVENT_DATE = '2020-01-02' (which equals MIN_DATE + 1).
• For player 2, the MIN_DATE is '2020-01-03' and there is no activity on '2020-01-04'.

Q1 will compute:
  SUM(case ...) = 2 (because both of player 1's qualifying rows count) and the denominator is COUNT(DISTINCT PLAYER_ID) = 2, giving a fraction of 1.00.

Q2 will perform a LEFT JOIN that returns two rows for player 1 (both join successfully) and one row for player 2 (with A.PLAYER_ID = NULL), so it yields:
  COUNT(A.PLAYER_ID) = 2 and COUNT(F.PLAYER_ID) = 3, giving a fraction of 0.67 (after rounding to 2 decimal places).

Thus, the two queries produce different results in this scenario.
*/

INSERT INTO SALESPERSON (SALES_ID, NAME, SALARY, COMMISSION_RATE, HIRE_DATE) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 50000, 10, '2020-01-01');

INSERT INTO SALESPERSON (SALES_ID, NAME, SALARY, COMMISSION_RATE, HIRE_DATE) VALUES (2, 'Bob', 60000, 15, '2019-05-15');

INSERT INTO COMPANY (COM_ID, NAME, CITY) VALUES (10, 'RED', 'New York');

INSERT INTO ORDERS (ORDER_ID, COM_ID, SALES_ID, ORDER_DATE, AMOUNT) VALUES (100, 10, NULL, '2021-03-03', 1000);

