counterexample:
-- Under the given schema no counterexample exists. 
-- For example, if we try the following test data the results of Q1 and Q2 coincide:

INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (2, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (3, 0);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (4, 1);

-- Explanation:
-- For SEAT_ID = 1, the right neighbor (seat 2) is free so Q1 returns 1. 
-- For SEAT_ID = 2, the left neighbor (seat 1) is free so Q1 returns 2.
-- For SEAT_ID = 4, neither seat 3 (which is not free) nor seat 5 (missing) qualifies, so Q1 does not return 4.
-- Q2 will only join seats 1 and 2 (and vice‐versa) so its distinct output is {1,2}.

-- Any instance that respects the constraints (unique seat ids and FREE being boolean) will have Q1 and Q2 agree. Thus, no counterexample (i.e. test data where the two queries return different results) can be produced under the given schema.

